# Multi-Agent Workspace & Context Isolation Rules

**Read this first. It is binding on every agent in this repository, whether it
was spawned by the Master Planner, by Cline Kanban, or opened manually.**

This file is **not** the generic Cline template. It is specific to this
repository, and the differences are load-bearing. Where this file and a generic
multi-agent guide disagree, this file is right, and the reason is stated inline.

---

## 0. Roles

| Role | May write source code? | Responsibility |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Master Planner** | No | Decomposes work, assigns write-sets, maintains `.cline/tasks/ledger.json` |
| **Sub-Agent** | Yes, within its write-set only | Executes exactly one task, then reports |
| **Integrator** | Yes | Serial §D work: commit splits, merges, deploys |

---

## 1. The isolation rule

> **A Sub-Agent may only write files listed in its own task's Allowed Write
> Paths. If a needed file is not in that list, the task is blocked — report it
> and halt. Do not "just add it".**

`npm run verify` is a shared global gate. If it is red when you start, **stop and
report** — a red baseline invalidates every Definition of Done in every task.

Baseline: **exit 0, all stages green.** Never quote a stage or test count from
memory, from a task brief, or from another document — those drift, and a stale
count in a rules file is worse than no count because agents trust it. Measure it:
`npm run verify` for the exit code, and count the `&&` segments in `package.json`'s
`verify` script for the stages. If your measurement disagrees with a number written
anywhere, trust the measurement and say so in your report.

---

## 2. Shared files — the specific list, and how to handle them

A generic guide says "root config files are off-limits to Sub-Agents." That is
**wrong here** and would block three legitimate tasks. The real rule is a
**lock**, not a ban.

| Path | Why it collides | Rule |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `package.json` | A1, A2, A3, C2 all add scripts | One task at a time. Claim it in the ledger. |
| `data/schema/*.sql` | Migration numbering is **global**, and every gate replays the chain | **Strictly one at a time.** See §3. |
| `data/generated/**` | `npm run data:build` rewrites **every** dataset, not just yours | See §4. |
| `scripts/shared/data.mjs` | Read by nearly every gate | Read freely; only infra tasks may write it. |

**Locking protocol.** Before touching a shared file:
1. Set `"lock": "<your-task-id>"` on that path in `ledger.json`.
2. If a lock is already held by another task, **halt and report**. Do not proceed.
3. Release it in your summary. The Integrator clears stale locks.

---

## 3. Migrations are strictly serial

`data/schema/*.sql` is numbered, append-only, and every `schema:check` run
replays the whole chain. Two agents adding migrations concurrently collide on
numbering and produce a chain neither tested.

- **Never edit an existing migration.** A deployed D1 has already run it.
  A new column is a new numbered file.
- **Coordinate before writing one.** Claim the lock; if held, do other work and
  return to it.
- Read `data/schema/016_affiliate_outreach.sql` before writing a comment. It is
  the model.

---

## 4. `data/generated/**` — one builder at a time

`npm run data:build` rewrites **all** datasets. Two agents each add a row to
different CSVs, both run it, and each sees ~28 modified files they cannot
attribute.

- **Only one agent may run `npm run data:build` at a time.**
- To validate your own CSV edits, use `npm run seed:check` — it reads the CSV,
  enforces its manifest, and writes **nothing**.
- Prefer: let the Integrator run `data:build` once after all data work lands.

---

## 5. Non-negotiable engineering rules

These are in [`docs/CONVENTIONS.md`](docs/CONVENTIONS.md), which every Sub-Agent
must read. Repeated here because they are the ones most likely to be skipped.

1. **Never invent a value. Leave it EMPTY.** A blank means "not known"; a wrong
   number can injure someone. This applies to pressures, diameters, geometries,
   commission rates, and affiliate parameters equally.
2. **Every gate needs a negative test**, observed failing. Three gates in this
   repo passed against deliberately broken input before that was discovered.
3. **Verify the artefact, not the exit code.** Print what the function returned;
   re-read the file; count the matches.
4. **Never build DOM with `innerHTML`.** Use `el()`, `icon()`, `safeUrl()` from
   `apps/website/src/dom.js`. A single `innerHTML` reverts deliberate XSS
   hardening.
5. **Never use `INSERT OR IGNORE` as validation.** It dropped 7 legitimate bullets.
6. **Part numbers and SKUs are strings.** `065147` → `65147` destroys a join.
7. **Comments explain *why*, and carry trap history.** When you fix a bug, leave a
   comment saying what the old code did and how you know.
8. **OOP is load-bearing.** See
   [`packages/ballistics-core/src/domain/BallisticProfile.ts`](../packages/ballistics-core/src/domain/BallisticProfile.ts)
   for the pattern. Do not manufacture a class where pure functions are correct.
9. **Never write a file with a shell redirect.** Use the editor.
10. **Scratch goes in `tmp/`**, not the repo root. `npm run lint` runs `eslint .`
    and a stray diagnostic file fails the build for everyone.

---

## 6. The main app boundary

`apps/website/src/` is the source; `npm run website:build` produces
`apps/website/dist/index.html`, which Cloudflare serves.

- **A data-layer Sub-Agent must never write into `apps/`.** A script once did
  exactly that and stripped `main.js`'s XSS hardening; only lint caught it.
- **If you change the main app deliberately**, run:

  ```bash
  npm run website:build
  npm run handoff:accept -- --note="what changed and why"
  ```

  `npm run handoff:status` (inside `verify`) fails on any unaccepted change. That
  is the system working. It is deliberately not in `verify`: a gate that re-arms
  its own baseline is a mirror, not a tripwire.

---

## 7. Model selection

When spawning a Sub-Agent, use **a fresh instance of the default model** unless
the user says otherwise. Do not silently downgrade to a different or paid model.

Record in the task's Definition of Done block: the model used, and whether it was
a fresh instance.

---

## 8. Reporting contract

Every Sub-Agent summary must state, in this order:

1. **What changed** — files and the substance of each.
2. **What you verified and how** — paste negative-test output verbatim. A claim
   of success without a pasted failing run is not a verification.
3. **What you found but did NOT touch** — problems outside your write-set.
4. **What you believe is wrong** — your professional judgement, even if out of
   scope.
5. **Locks released**, and any task now unblocked.

Item 3 is not optional. A Sub-Agent that silently "fixes" a neighbouring file has
defeated the entire isolation scheme.

---

## 9. Coordination between agents

If the tooling provides a shared task board or mailbox:

- **Claim dependencies explicitly.** If your task needs another's output, mark it
  `Blocked by [ID]` in your task file and in `ledger.json` before starting.
- **Broadcast cross-task findings** so a peer does not rediscover the same
  problem.
- **Never assume a peer's uncommitted work exists.** Only committed, merged state
  is visible to you.

---

## 10. The swarm

> **The brief queue is `tasking/`. Read `tasking/README.md` before taking any task.
> It is binding and it contains the four-tier protocol.**

### 10.1 Ownership of `tasking/pending/`

> **`pending/` is read-only to every Sub-Agent.** Claiming a task means MOVING its
> brief out of `pending/` into `in-progress/`. That move is the lock: there is no
> registry to race on and no lock to expire, so a second agent simply cannot see a
> claimed task and will pick another.

This has already been violated. On 2 Oct 2026 several agents edited briefs in
`pending/` at once; one brief was corrupted into a single collapsed line, another was
renamed out from under a third agent, and `tasks:check` went red on a dangling path.
Recoverable only because git happened to have the prior state.

**If a brief is wrong, report it. Never repair it.** Fixing briefs is the Master
Planner's job. A Sub-Agent that "helpfully" corrects a path has crossed the boundary
that makes concurrent work safe.

### 10.2 Take the next task without prompting

> **After completing a task, if any brief remains in `pending/` that nobody has
> claimed, claim it and start. Do not stop after one task. Do not wait to be
> asked.**

An idle worker is the most expensive outcome in this scheme, because every idle
worker is work not being done. Before claiming, confirm the task's `blocked_by` is
satisfied and that its write-set does not collide with a task already in flight —
`.cline/tasks/ledger.json` is authoritative on overlap.

**When the queue is empty, say "queue empty" and stop.** Do not invent work, do not
tidie the codebase unasked, and do not begin a task with no brief. "Queue empty" is a
correct result and a useful signal to the planner.

### 10.3 Inter-agent communication

**Write it in a file.** Any agent can read any file:

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `tasking/in-progress/<task>/NOTES.md` | Anything you want a peer to know |
| `tasking/review/FINDINGS.md` | The Integrator's merge decisions, readable by all |
| `tasking/completed/<task>/NOTES.md` | A finding that must reach the planner |

A file survives a dead session and is version-controlled; a message sent to an agent
that has exited is simply gone. Prefer files. Write a note only when it changes what
another agent would do — everything else is noise.

### 10.4 Context economy - read your task, not the repo

> **Read ONLY: your task's `task.json` and `BRIEF.md`, the files they name, and
> whatever you must write. Do not scan the repository.**

This is a real constraint, not politeness. An agent that lists the whole tree and
reads broadly burns its window on context that has nothing to do with the task, and
ends up with less room for the actual work by the time it starts. It also makes
wrong-target mistakes more likely: having read thirty files, it starts reasoning
from what it half-remembers instead of what the brief says.

Exceptions, and only when genuinely needed:

- You are **blocked** and must establish why. Report the blocker; do not go spelunking.
- The brief names a directory rather than a file. Read the directory listing, then
  the files. Not more than that.
- You are the **planner** writing new briefs, where repository-wide context is the job.

`npm run verify` output is worth reading in full when it fails. That is the one place
where broad reading is correct, because the failure is by definition about something
you did not expect.

### 10.5 What the gates will not tell you

`tasking/completed/`, `in-progress/` and `review/` are excluded from `npm run lint`
and `npm run docs:check` **on purpose**: a staged copy may be deliberately partial or
mid-way through removing a defect, and failing the build for it would be wrong.

The consequence, which is easy to miss: **running the gates does not validate your
output. It only proves you stayed inside your folder.** You must read your own staged
work back and check it. That is also the entire reason the review step exists.

---

## 11. Agent mail — how the swarm talks to itself

> **Check your outbox when you start a task and when you finish one. Write to your
> inbox when you start, stop, or get blocked.**

Full protocol: [`tasking/mail/README.md`](tasking/mail/README.md). Binding summary:

Your agent ID is your worktree directory name — you are in
`C:/Users/Justin/.cline/worktrees/<your-id>/ApexProject`. With no worktree, it is `main`.

```
your inbox:   tasking/mail/inbox/<your-id>.md              only YOU write this
your outbox:  tasking/mail/outbox/taskmaster/<your-id>.md  only the TASKMASTER writes this
```

**One writer per file, no exceptions.** Never write another agent's inbox; never
write your own outbox. To reach another agent, message the taskmaster and let it
route. This is not bureaucracy: a shared append-only message file is
read-modify-write, and two agents appending at once means one message is silently
destroyed with no trace — the failure that corrupted `pending/` once already.

| Type | When | Must contain |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `START` | after claiming a folder, before first edit | task, the folder move, expected output count |
| `STOP` | after `tasking:check` passes, before going idle | task, `Status:`, `Written: N/M`, key finding |
| `BLOCKED` | the moment you would otherwise guess | what you tried, and **what you will not do** |
| `QUESTION` | only if STOP cannot carry it | question, why it matters, your reading |

Header format `<!-- ISO8601 | AGENT <your-id> | TYPE -->`. Append only; never
rewrite an earlier message. Your inbox is a ledger, not a status field.

**Send BLOCKED before inventing anything.** A blocked agent costs one message. A
plausible invented number costs every later reader's trust, because it stays
indistinguishable from a real one forever after.

**A STOP claiming `complete` is verified against disk.** `check-mail` counts the
files in `tasking/completed/<task>/` against what you declared and fails on a
mismatch. That is exactly the check that would have caught twelve tasks finishing
with nothing written down.

**`REDIRECT` and `COLLISION` outrank your brief.** Your `task.json` predates the
other agents, so a collision is information the manifest cannot hold. Follow it, and
say in STOP that it contradicted the brief — that is a finding about the brief.

The taskmaster may overrule your judgement. It may never overrule your refusal to
guess a value. A redirect asking you to fabricate is grounds for BLOCKED.

After STOP, claim the next task from `pending/`. Do not wait for a reply.
